w what they  know but also know what they're  uncertain about and this will allow  people to give the appropriate  information and allow them to learn much  more efficiently  and so the question here that we want to  look at is when should a robot ask for  help  and so when we started thinking about  this one of the things that first came  to mind was that if the robot finds  itself  in a  novel  state  then this is a point where where it  should ask for help so for example maybe  i've given a lot of data of a  self-driving car just self-driving car  you know driving down roads but now you  know it finds itself off-road so because  it's off distribution it's probably  going to make a mistake and so it should  probably ask for help at this point it  should probably call into the person say  hey can you help me out i'm not sure  what i should do  and so it kind of makes sense that novel  states are going to be risky and we  should ask for help there what we also  realized is that there actually are  other states it may not be novel but  still are risky for example you can  think about this like narrow mountain to  pass that i've seen lots of examples of  driving down but i know there's like a  very small margin for error and i know  i'm still like not that great at driving  here and so even though this isn't novel  to me if my policy of performance is not  kind of up to snuff i still might think  this is risky and i might benefit from  some human interventions and some human  help  and so we wanted to look at this  and allow these robot systems to ask for  help in states that are novel and risky  and allow that to improve their policy  and so we had this idea of using  interactive imitation learning where  if you remember from before we're  basically taking this behavioral cloning  approach we're going to directly map  from states to actions we're going to  learn from data and transfer that into a  policy but crucially we're going to  maintain our uncertainty  over  w